WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.761 --> 00:00:19.882
we can link this man Beppo to three busts," said Holmes as we left Morse Hudson's shop.

2
00:00:20.763 --> 00:00:24.204
"Now, Watson, let's head for Gelder and Company, the sculpture works where the busts were made."

3
00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:26.745
We found the factory down by the waterfront.

4
00:00:27.526 --> 00:00:29.326
Outside was a yard filled with sculptures.

5
00:00:30.247 --> 00:00:33.288
Inside was a large room where fifty workers were carving or molding.

6
00:00:34.188 --> 00:00:38.551
The manager checked his ledgers and said that hundreds of copies had been made of the Napoleon sculpture.

7
00:00:39.512 --> 00:00:43.955
About a year ago, six had been made; three were sold to Morse Hudson's shop and three to

8
00:00:43.975 --> 00:00:44.976
the Harding Brothers shop.

9
00:00:44.996 --> 00:00:47.758
"Was there anything different about those six busts?"

10
00:00:48.218 --> 00:00:48.798
asked Holmes.

11
00:00:49.759 --> 00:00:51.360
"No." The manager began to laugh.

12
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.422
"I don't understand why anyone would want to steal them.

13
00:00:55.163 --> 00:00:56.904
They're just cheap plaster copies."

14
00:00:57.925 --> 00:01:02.168
He explained that the cast was made in two molds from each side of the statue's face.

15
00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.361
Then those two profiles were joined together to complete the bust.

16
00:01:07.302 --> 00:01:10.223
Italian immigrants usually did the work in the room where we were standing.

17
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:15.165
When finished, the busts were put on a table to dry, and then they were stored until sold.

18
00:01:15.185 --> 00:01:17.706
"That's all I can tell you," said the manager.

19
00:01:18.626 --> 00:01:21.487
Up until this point, he had calmly answered all Holmes' questions.

20
00:01:22.408 --> 00:01:26.429
But when Holmes showed him the photograph of Beppo, a remarkable change came over the manager.

21
00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.570
His face flushed with anger.

22
00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.371
"Ah, the rascal!"

23
00:01:30.671 --> 00:01:31.151
he cried.

24
00:01:31.171 --> 00:01:33.052
"Yes indeed, I know him well.

25
00:01:33.888 --> 00:01:38.271
This has always been a respectable business, and the only time we've had the police here was because

26
00:01:38.291 --> 00:01:38.892
of this fellow.

27
00:01:39.672 --> 00:01:40.833
It was more than a year ago now."

28
00:01:41.714 --> 00:01:44.876
The manager explained that Beppo had knifed another Italian man in the street.

29
00:01:45.717 --> 00:01:48.359
Then he fled to the sculpture factory, where the police arrested him.

30
00:01:48.379 --> 00:01:51.241
"How long a prison sentence did he get?"

31
00:01:51.621 --> 00:01:52.021
I asked.

32
00:01:52.882 --> 00:01:56.424
"The man he knifed didn't die, so Beppo was sentenced to only a year in prison," said the

33
00:01:56.444 --> 00:02:00.948
manager. "I have no doubt he's out now, but he hasn't dared to show his face around here.

34
00:02:01.812 --> 00:02:04.614
One of his cousins works for us and I suppose that man could tell you where he is."

35
00:02:04.634 --> 00:02:05.994
"No, no!"

36
00:02:06.315 --> 00:02:06.875
cried Holmes.

37
00:02:06.895 --> 00:02:10.056
"Not a word to the cousin, not a word to anyone.

38
00:02:10.857 --> 00:02:14.819
This case is very important, and the farther I go with it, the more important it seems to

39
00:02:14.859 --> 00:02:19.321
grow. When you checked your ledgers for the sale of those six sculptures, I noticed that the date

40
00:02:19.361 --> 00:02:20.622
was June 3rd last year.

41
00:02:21.343 --> 00:02:23.203
Could you tell me the date when Beppo was arrested?"

42
00:02:23.224 --> 00:02:25.505
"I could tell you roughly by the pay list."

43
00:02:26.265 --> 00:02:28.266
The manager turned some pages in another record book.

44
00:02:29.047 --> 00:02:31.068
"Yes, we paid his final wages on May 30th."

45
00:02:32.316 --> 00:02:33.596
"Thank you," said Holmes.

46
00:02:33.616 --> 00:02:37.558
"I don't think that I need to intrude on your time and patience anymore."

47
00:02:38.398 --> 00:02:41.539
At this point we left the factory and searched out a restaurant for a late lunch.

48
00:02:42.339 --> 00:02:45.100
We passed a newsboy hawking the latest edition of the newspaper.

49
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.001
A sign at his feet announced the front page headline: "Kensington Outrage.

50
00:02:50.901 --> 00:02:52.062
Maniac Commits Murder."

51
00:02:52.942 --> 00:02:55.123
Holmes bought a copy of the paper and read it while we ate.

52
00:02:55.903 --> 00:02:59.624
"Mr. Horace Harker has managed to write something after all," he said with a smile.

53
00:03:00.552 --> 00:03:05.173
"Harker says here that Lestrade and I agree that the murderer is a homicidal maniac with a fixation

54
00:03:05.233 --> 00:03:05.793
on Napoleon.

55
00:03:06.634 --> 00:03:09.334
The press can be a useful ally, Watson, if you know how to use it.

56
00:03:10.055 --> 00:03:12.895
This false clue will make the murderer feel that we are off his trail.

57
00:03:13.796 --> 00:03:17.877
And now, if you've finished eating, we'll return to Harding Brothers to ask Harding about the three Napoleon

58
00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:18.937
busts that he purchased."

59
00:03:19.857 --> 00:03:20.517
This time Mr.

60
00:03:20.557 --> 00:03:21.738
Harding was in when we called.

61
00:03:23.178 --> 00:03:25.419
He had already read the account of the murder in newspapers.

62
00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:29.860
By consulting his ledger, he was able to tell us that he'd sold one bust to Mr.

63
00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:31.164
Harker, one to Mr.

64
00:03:31.205 --> 00:03:32.986
Brown in Chiswick, and one to Mr.

65
00:03:33.006 --> 00:03:33.846
Sandeford in Reading.

66
00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:36.228
Holmes showed him Beppo's photograph.

67
00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:39.570
"No, I've never seen that man before," said Mr.

68
00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.512
Harding. "Do you have any Italian immigrants on your staff?"

69
00:03:42.893 --> 00:03:43.413
asked Holmes.

70
00:03:43.433 --> 00:03:45.755
"And would they have access to that ledger?"

71
00:03:45.775 --> 00:03:49.137
"Yes, we have several Italians among our workers.

72
00:03:50.018 --> 00:03:53.100
I guess they might take a look at that ledger if they wanted to; there's nothing to stop

73
00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.381
them." Holmes had made some notes while Mr.

74
00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:59.004
Harding talked, and I could see that the detective was satisfied by the way things were going.

75
00:03:59.912 --> 00:04:03.855
As we left the shop, however, he said nothing except that we had to hurry or we'd be

76
00:04:03.895 --> 00:04:05.516
late for our appointment with Lestrade.

77
00:04:06.997 --> 00:04:10.560
Sure enough when we reached Baker Street, we found Lestrade impatiently pacing our sitting room.

78
00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.842
I could tell from his expression that he was bursting to tell us something.

79
00:04:13.862 --> 00:04:15.923
"Have you had any luck today, Mr.

80
00:04:15.943 --> 00:04:17.284
Holmes?" asked Lestrade.

81
00:04:17.304 --> 00:04:20.867
"I can trace each of the busts now from the manufacturer to the owner."

82
00:04:21.748 --> 00:04:22.448
"The busts!"

83
00:04:22.868 --> 00:04:23.509
cried Lestrade.

84
00:04:24.229 --> 00:04:27.311
"It's not for me to criticize your methods, but I think I've done a better day's work than

85
00:04:27.352 --> 00:04:27.752
you have.

86
00:04:28.752 --> 00:04:31.373
I've identified the dead man and found a motive for the crime!"

87
00:04:31.393 --> 00:04:33.613
"Really?" said Holmes.

88
00:04:34.514 --> 00:04:39.275
"Splendid!" "We have an inspector who knows the Italian Quarter well," explained Lestrade.

89
00:04:39.295 --> 00:04:42.296
"Inspector Hill recognized the dead man right away.

90
00:04:42.996 --> 00:04:46.937
His name is Pietro Venucci, and he's one of the most vicious murderers in London."

91
00:04:46.957 --> 00:04:50.218
"Venucci," Holmes said quietly to himself.

92
00:04:51.738 --> 00:04:56.780
Lestrade's theory was that the man in the photograph was probably another Italian whom Venucci wanted to kill

93
00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:57.440
for some reason.

94
00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:02.102
"Venucci had the photograph so that he could identify the man to be killed; he wouldn't want to

95
00:05:02.122 --> 00:05:03.882
knife the wrong person," said Lestrade.

96
00:05:03.902 --> 00:05:08.964
"After seeing the man climb through the window at Harker's house, Venucci waited outside for him.

97
00:05:09.785 --> 00:05:13.546
When the man came out, they scuffled and Venucci was the one who ended up dead."

98
00:05:14.407 --> 00:05:15.467
Holmes clapped his hands.

99
00:05:15.487 --> 00:05:17.248
"Excellent, Lestrade!

100
00:05:17.948 --> 00:05:20.269
But I don't quite see where the busts fit into your theory."

101
00:05:20.289 --> 00:05:21.790
"The busts!

102
00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.211
Why can't you get those out of your head?

103
00:05:25.011 --> 00:05:27.212
I'm investigating a murder here, not a petty crime.

104
00:05:28.128 --> 00:05:30.670
Come down to the Italian Quarter with me and we'll find the murderer."

105
00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:34.753
"I think the place to find the man will be Chiswick," said Holmes.

106
00:05:34.773 --> 00:05:39.436
"Come with me to Chiswick tonight, and if my plan fails, I'll go to the Italian Quarter with

107
00:05:39.456 --> 00:05:40.016
you tomorrow.

108
00:05:40.897 --> 00:05:42.958
Meanwhile, Watson, ring for an express messenger.

109
00:05:43.259 --> 00:05:45.460
I have an important letter that must be sent right away."

110
00:05:46.321 --> 00:05:50.724
Holmes did not intend to leave for Chiswick before eleven o'clock, so he offered Lestrade supper and a

111
00:05:50.764 --> 00:05:52.085
place to nap for a few hours.

112
00:05:53.165 --> 00:05:56.548
In the meantime he spent the evening rummaging through his files of old newspapers.

113
00:05:57.504 --> 00:06:00.265
When he finished, he had a look of triumph in his eyes but he said nothing.

114
00:06:01.085 --> 00:06:04.386
For my part, I remembered that one of the two remaining busts was sold to a man in

115
00:06:04.406 --> 00:06:09.128
Chiswick. The purpose of our late night journey was undoubtedly to catch the thief in the act of

116
00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:10.108
stealing another bust.

117
00:06:11.048 --> 00:06:13.709
Later, after we stepped into the cab, Holmes turned to me.

118
00:06:13.729 --> 00:06:16.310
"I do hope you brought your revolver, Watson."
